<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Plaisance</h1><p class="st-meta">1662 CE – 1713 CE · capital Plaisance</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's fortified fishing colony on the south coast of Newfoundland, which raided English settlements before being ceded to Britain in 1713.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French fishing crews had long used the sheltered harbour of Plaisance, now Placentia, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avalon-peninsula">Avalon Peninsula</a>. French claims to the harbour date from the 1650s, but the colony is usually dated from 1662, when a garrison and the first resident governor arrived, making Plaisance the administrative centre of French <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Plaisance was a small military and fishing post with a few hundred residents and many more seasonal fishermen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a> and the Basque coast. Its governors, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-francois-de-brouillan">Jacques-François de Brouillan</a>, oversaw Fort Saint-Louis and the dry cod trade. In 1696 to 1697 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a> led raids from Plaisance that destroyed most English settlements on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-newfoundland-and-labrador">St John's</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> in 1713 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, keeping only fishing rights on part of the coast. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-pastour-de-costebelle">Philippe Pastour de Costebelle</a> moved the settlers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-royale">Ile Royale</a>, and Placentia became a British garrison town in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-newfoundland">Colony of Newfoundland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1662 CE – 1713 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.78%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1662 CE – 1713 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Plaisance</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jacques-François de Brouillan, Philippe Pastour de Costebelle</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Canada</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Newfoundland, Placentia</dd></dl></aside></div>
